A good employee-records datastructure should embody **Rights** in its structure: it must clearly support (1) **who** is entitled to see or act on which parts of the data, (2) **what claims/attributes** are held by an employee, and (3) **how access, changes, and ownership** are tracked so entitlements aren’t confused or overwritten.

## Datastructure: Access-Controlled Employee Record Store

### 1) Core record (the “Claim set”)
Use an **Employee record** as a structured object containing:
- `employee_id` (unique identifier)
- `identity_claims`: { name, legal_status, DOB (if appropriate), etc. }
- `employment_claims`: { title, department, manager_id, start_date, etc. }
- `compensation_claims`: { salary, bonus eligibility, pay grade }
- `contact_claims`: { work_email, phone }
- `status_claims`: { active, terminated_date }
- `audit_metadata`: creation/modification times, version number

Mechanically, this is a Rights-aligned **partition of information into claim-types**. That partition is what later lets you grant access to some claim-types while withholding others.

### 2) Store (fast lookup by entitlement-relevant key)
Use a **hash map / dictionary**:
- `employees: Map<EmployeeID, EmployeeRecord>`

